Egypt's oldest love story is not about a perfect couple. It is about a love that would not let the other be lost.

For three thousand years, Egyptians told of **Isis and Osiris**: a king who was killed, a wife who searched the whole land for him, and a child born of their union who became king. It sat at the heart of their faith, their funerals and their idea of kingship.

It still says something true about two people who choose each other.

## The story, as the Egyptians told it

**Osiris** was a good king. A hymn carved in the New Kingdom, now in the Louvre, remembers him as the rightful heir of earth and sky, who ruled with justice.

His brother **Set** wanted his throne, and killed him. The Egyptian texts barely describe the murder. It was, it seems, too terrible to be told.

Then **Isis** went searching. With her sister **Nephthys**, she found Osiris and mourned him. In the oldest texts the two sisters are pictured as birds, crying over him. **Anubis**, god of embalming, prepared his body, the model for every Egyptian burial after.

You may have heard more: the beautiful chest Set tricked him into, the body thrown into the Nile and carried to Byblos, the fourteen pieces scattered across Egypt. Those details come from **Plutarch**, a Greek writer, around AD 100. He wrote the only full version of the story we have. Later Egyptian temples did honour a relic of Osiris in many different cities, so the idea of a body scattered and gathered was real to them too.

## She would not let him be lost

What the Egyptians loved most about this story was Isis.

She did not accept the loss. She searched, she mourned, and she used everything she knew. They called her **"Great of Magic"**, and the Great Hymn to Osiris pictures her bending over him and, in one old translation, **"with her wings created air."** With that breath, he came back to her for long enough. From their union came a son, **Horus**.

Osiris did not return to rule on earth. He became king of the afterlife, the one every Egyptian hoped to join. Their son grew up to face Set and take back what was his.

> She would not let him be lost.

![Columns crowned with the face of a goddess at the temple of Dendera](/images/articles/isis-osiris-dendera.jpg)
*At Dendera, north of Luxor, chapels on the roof once held the mysteries of Osiris's return.*

## From their union, a king

Here is the part that turns a love story into the foundation of a country.

Isis's name is written in hieroglyphs with the sign of a **throne**, and she wears that throne on her head. Many scholars link her to the throne itself. One of her titles, recorded in temples, calls her the one **"without whose consent no king ascends the throne."**

Every living pharaoh was seen as **Horus**, the child of Isis and Osiris. Every king who died became **Osiris**. So the story was told again with every reign: a union, an heir, order restored after loss.

The Egyptians did not see kingship as one man's power. They saw it as something born of two, and protected by the one who never gave up.

![Ramesses III carved in the form of Osiris at Medinet Habu](/images/articles/medinet-habu-meaning-osiris-pillars.jpg)
*At Medinet Habu, Ramesses III is carved as Osiris, the king who rose again.*

## What it can still mean for two people

This is our reflection, not an ancient teaching. But it is hard to read this story and not see a couple in it.

- **Love that sees the other whole.** Isis gathered what had been broken and saw the whole person when he could not. The best partners still do that for each other.
- **Love that builds.** Their union did not end with the two of them. It made an heir, a throne, a kingdom. A strong couple still builds something larger than itself: a home, a family, a life's work.
- **Love that stays through loss.** The story begins with grief. What makes it a love story is what she did next.
- **Love that is renewed.** Each year, in the month of Khoiak, Egyptians planted grain in Osiris-shaped beds of earth and watched it sprout. Love, like that grain, was something you tended so it could grow again.

## The Egyptians wrote love songs too

Not all Egyptian love was divine. About 3,200 years ago, people wrote love songs on papyrus and on pieces of pottery. Some were found at **Deir el-Medina**, the village of the tomb builders on Luxor's West Bank.

In them, lovers call each other "brother" and "sister", simply as words of tenderness. One line, in an old translation, reads: **"I see my sister coming and mine heart rejoiceth."**

Egyptian couples also had themselves carved together in stone, sitting side by side, an arm around the other's shoulder. They wanted to be remembered as two.

![A private felucca on the Nile in the evening light](/images/articles/isis-osiris-felucca-deck.jpg)
*The Nile at the end of the day: the simplest hour in Luxor, and the one couples remember.*

## Where to find Isis and Osiris from Luxor

- **Deir el-Shelwit, Luxor's West Bank.** A small temple to Isis from the Roman period, its gate carved under emperors of the first century AD and its sanctuary decorated under Hadrian. It was conserved and opened to visitors in 2014, and almost no one goes.
- **Medinet Habu, Luxor's West Bank.** Ramesses III stands carved as Osiris along its courts. We wrote about [why the Egyptians believed the world began there](/insiders-guide/medinet-habu-meaning).
- **Karnak, Luxor.** The Temple of Opet holds a symbolic tomb of Osiris, where the god was believed to be reborn. Small chapels of Osiris, built with the God's Wives of Amun, stand in the north of the precinct.
- **Dendera and Abydos, a long day north.** Dendera's rooftop chapels of Osiris; Abydos, Osiris's holiest place for thousands of years.
- **Philae, near Aswan.** The island temple of Isis, where her statue sailed every tenth day to visit the tomb of Osiris. Best with a night in Aswan. Ancient writers explained its name as "friendship".

## A day for two

Most of the couples who write to us are marking something: an anniversary, a honeymoon, a milestone, a question they are planning to ask.

That is what our **Signature** day was made for. It is private and arranged end to end, built around the two of you, not around a bus timetable. It might begin in the hidden temple of Isis at the quiet hour, when there is no one else inside. Then a table where locals actually eat, the temples at their quietest, and a felucca on the Nile as the light turns gold.

If you want a moment of your own, a proposal, a renewed promise, a surprise, we plan it quietly beforehand and time it to the light. The other person suspects nothing until it happens.

![A felucca sailing on the Nile at sunset](/images/articles/isis-osiris-felucca-sunset.jpg)
*Some promises are best made on the water.*

## What most people miss

Deir el-Shelwit. It sits a few kilometres south of Medinet Habu, small and quiet, and most visitors to Luxor never hear of it. For a couple, it may be the most fitting place in the city: a temple to the goddess who would not let love be lost.

If you would like to begin your day there, [this is how we arrange it](/concierge-day).
